<article class="env-definition" id="024N">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/024N">Definition <span data-tag="024N">41.3.5</span>.</a> (See Morphisms, Definition <a href="/tag/02G4" data-tag="02G4">29.36.1</a> for the definition in the general case.) Let $Y$ be a locally Noetherian scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be locally of finite type. Let $x \in X$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We say $f$ is <em>unramified at $x$</em> if $\mathcal{O}_{Y, f(x)} \to \mathcal{O}_{X, x}$ is an unramified homomorphism of local rings. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The morphism $f : X \to Y$ is said to be <em>unramified</em> if it is unramified at all points of $X$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>